+\section{How We Constructed Non-Official Test Cases}
+\textbf{Motivation.} Repeatedly modifying a synthesizer after checking the same 25 Stage 1 inputs risks learning incidental features of those particular Boolean tables. More importantly, the public set alone cannot tell us whether an optimization helps affine parity-like functions, mux-heavy structures, sparse products, shared outputs, or dense random truth tables equally. We therefore constructed additional functions from \emph{generative descriptions} and only then expanded them into complete truth tables. This is not a new scoring system, and these cases are not professor-provided Stage 2 data.
+
+\textbf{Construction.} A deterministic generator chooses a function family, a fixed pseudorandom seed, $n$ input variables, $m$ outputs, legal gate prices, and a delay target. For each assignment $x\in\{0,1\}^n$ it evaluates every output and serializes the resulting $m$-bit row. Thus the generator produces a legal complete instance, not a hand-selected Netlist. The mixed family includes affine functions (parity of selected inputs), sparse SOP, threshold functions, selector-based MUX, arithmetic bits, shared intermediate predicates, and dense deterministic truth bits. A separate large-function family composes local lookup tables, XORs, shared ensembles, and cascaded multiplexors. The scripts and case-hash manifests are preserved in \texttt{shared/test\_generation/}; the original truth tables are retained separately rather than redistributed in a public writing package.
+
+\textbf{Evaluation protocol.} The Round 5.1 test record has an 80-case mixed set and a 16-case large structured set, plus 110 older regression cases and two stress cases. Round 6.1 was additionally evaluated on a newly seeded 40-case mixed set and an 8-case large set after its budget was selected. In contrast, the 25/35/45-ms cut-search budget sweep was conducted on development sets; it should not be mislabeled an untouched holdout. Each run checks functionality, legality, target delay, area, and synthesis time against the appropriate frozen comparator. A deterministic seed makes a suite repeatable but does not make it representative of an unknown deployment distribution.
+
+\textbf{Research lesson.} A regression suite answers \emph{did this change break previously checked behavior?}; a frozen generated holdout asks \emph{does the promising trend persist on a different specified family?}; only the later official Stage 2 release will answer the separate question of generalization to the next course distribution. These distinctions stop an attractive Stage 1 result from being exaggerated into a universal claim.
 
 \section{How did we guard against benchmark overfitting?}
 A public benchmark is a finite development set. Repeatedly adjusting thresholds until its aggregate area decreases risks overfitting the properties of those 25 released functions. We therefore distinguish \emph{development evidence}, \emph{repeated regression tests}, and \emph{fresh generated holdouts} frozen after the candidate choices. Functions were generated from affine, sparse, threshold, multiplexing, arithmetic, dense and structured graph-related families across different sizes. Fresh holdout results were not used for further parameter tuning in the corresponding finalized experiments.
